     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-08 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge quick">quick bite</span></div>
       <h1>Brown University Engineers Develop All-Electrical Cell Imaging Technique</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Engineers at Brown University have developed an electrical imaging method to track cell migration without traditional optical microscopes. Published in the journal Lab on a Chip, the technique uses a microchip array of 1 million electrodes to monitor cells in a liquid nutrient solution.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-08/brown-university-engineers-develop-all-electrical-cell-imagi.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>By measuring changes in electrical capacitance as cells move across the chip, researchers can observe dynamics like cancer metastasis and division without damaging cells with fluorescent dyes. This approach avoids the potential behavioral disruption and DNA damage often associated with optical and fluorescence-based imaging technologies.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Excellent density; covers the technical mechanism and the specific benefits identified in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-electrical-imaging-alternative-tracking-cell.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
