     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-10-08 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Election Commission Faces Criticism Over Electoral Roll Deletions</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The Election Commission of India (ECI), led by Gyanesh Kumar, is under scrutiny following a Special Intensive Revision (SIR) of electoral rolls that resulted in the deletion of 13.37 crore names across 30 States and Union Territories. Critics argue that methodological flaws and ad-hoc rule changes have disenfranchised millions of genuine voters, with deletion rates reaching 17% in the third phase of the revision.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-08/election-commission-faces-criticism-over-electoral-roll-dele.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>In some regions, such as Delhi, current draft rolls contain fewer electors than the number of votes cast in the 2025 Assembly election. The ECI has since initiated a special drive to re-enrol missing voters, implicitly acknowledging the systemic exclusion caused by the SIR process.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article effectively synthesizes complex data regarding the ECI's electoral roll revision process while maintaining necessary brevity.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/opinion/lead/flawed-by-design-exclusionary-in-impact/article71560731.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
