       <h1>EU Envoy Heads to Beijing Amid Trade Deficit Tensions</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#world</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>European Union trade envoy Maros Sefcovic arrived in Beijing on October 8, 2026, for talks aimed at addressing a $410 billion trade deficit. EU leaders express growing anxiety over Chinese state subsidies and exports, which they view as a threat to core industries including steel and automotive manufacturing. </p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-08/eu-envoy-heads-to-beijing-amid-trade-deficit-tensions.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>European Parliament lawmakers recently passed a resolution demanding economic reciprocity and a potential proportionate response to market imbalances. While the EU has already implemented measures against specific Chinese imports, officials are considering further actions, including potential tariffs, as they seek to protect European industrial sectors from underpriced competition.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief effectively captures the core trade tensions, specific deficit figures, and the policy context provided in the source.</p>
