     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · The Hindu World · 2026-10-08 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Italy’s Parliament Votes on Controversial Electoral Law Reform</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#world</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Italy’s Parliament is voting on a proposed electoral reform that would introduce a fully proportional system, granting a parliamentary majority to any alliance exceeding 42% of the vote. Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni’s government argues the change will ensure political stability, while opposition parties claim the reform is designed to favor the ruling coalition in next year's elections.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-08/italys-parliament-votes-on-controversial-electoral-law-refor.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The bill has already cleared the Senate and now faces a secret ballot in the lower house. Although Meloni has threatened to resign if the measure fails, analysts suggest the law is likely to pass, despite warnings from experts that it could inadvertently benefit the fractured opposition.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately summarizes the electoral reform proposal, the current political stakes, and the specific threshold mentioned.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/italy-parliament-votes-on-electoral-law-in-key-test-for-meloni/article71558577.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
