     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Mongabay India · 2026-10-08 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Moth and lichen association documented in Himachal Pradesh</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#environment</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Researchers have documented a moth using the umbilicate lichen, Lasallia pertusa, as a camouflage background, marking the first known record of this association. The observation occurred in July 2025 at 3,200 meters elevation in Himachal Pradesh's Kugti Wildlife Sanctuary.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-08/moth-and-lichen-association-documented-in-himachal-pradesh.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The findings, published in Arthropod-Plant Interactions, suggest that L. pertusa's complex, perforated thallus provides a specialized visual environment for camouflage. While the moth could not be identified to the species level due to the lack of a specimen, researchers believe this interaction may hold evolutionary significance. The study expands the range of lichen growth forms recognized for supporting insect camouflage strategies.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief is accurate, concise, and effectively captures all key details from the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://india.mongabay.com/short-article/2026/10/study-identifies-rare-case-of-moth-and-lichen-association/" rel="noopener">Mongabay India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
