       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-08 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Teacher well-being linked to improved student outcomes</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>New research published in Educational Psychology Review reveals a significant connection between teacher well-being and student success. After analyzing 186 studies involving over 350,000 teachers globally, researchers found that high teacher satisfaction and self-efficacy correlate with increased student motivation and academic achievement. Conversely, teacher burnout, stress, and poor psychological health are linked to lower student performance.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-08/teacher-well-being-linked-to-improved-student-outcomes.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This study highlights the importance of addressing teacher working conditions beyond just retention efforts. With schools facing increasing strain from shortages and unmanageable workloads, experts emphasize that supporting teacher mental health is critical not only for educators but as a direct factor in how well students learn in the classroom.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief is concise, accurate, and captures the core findings of the provided research summary and text effectively.</p>
