     <article class="story single" data-beat="positive">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">POSITIVE</span> · The Better India · 2026-10-08 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Tutor Vinay Das Mobilizes 1,600 Kids to Clean 250 Delhi Spaces</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Delhi</span> <span>#Community</span> <span>#Education</span> <span>#Environment</span></div>
       <p>Every Sunday in north‑west Delhi’s Wazirpur, 40‑50 children from migrant working‑class families don gloves, masks, brooms and sacks to clean a nearby park or street corner, walking beside tutor Vinay Das, who lives with his wife, two children and mother in an eight‑foot‑by‑eight‑foot rented room. Das began offering free tuition in 2013, turned down a lecturer post, and now teaches around 300 children, some pursuing master’s degrees.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-08/tutor-vinay-das-mobilizes-1600-kids-to-clean-250-delhi-space.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Since 1 September 2018, when Das led 150 volunteers to clear a park piled with 12‑15 ft of garbage, his volunteers have cleaned roughly 250 parks, streets and public spaces. The movement now includes 1,600 children, though about 350 join any drive, and the team also plants saplings tended for at least three months. Alumni such as Shruti, who started in 2019 and now holds a master’s, say their families have shifted from opposition to support.</p><p>The volunteers collect discarded idols, immersing them in artificial ponds and painting warnings on peepul trees. At the Munak canal they have cleaned about three kilometres, removed over 2,500 idols and hauled a 30‑35 kg Shivling. Work carries risk: volunteers have found glass, needles and chemicals that once blistered Das’s hands. Funding comes from a fee of up to ₹600 per student (Class 1‑12), covering supplies, stipends and rent for a free library; in 2024 Das registered the NGO Swasti Das Seva Samiti.</p><p>Delhi generates 11,852 tonnes of waste daily but processes only 7,611 tonnes, leaving 4,241 tonnes for dumps, while fresh waste still arrives at 11,000‑12,000 tonnes per day. The effort has drawn attention from Chief Minister Rekha Gupta and surveys show 95 % of residents in cleaned areas become more aware and litter less. Das says his aim is for these children to carry similar campaigns wherever life takes them.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is a highly effective, dense summary of the provided source material, capturing all key statistics, figures, and personal narrative details without padding.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://thebetterindia.com/changemakers/delhi-tutor-vinay-das-children-clean-parks-wazirpur-12637405" rel="noopener">The Better India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       <div class="cityguide"><b>City guide</b><p>More on <a href="https://bharatdn.com/city/delhi/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Delhi</a> - census 2011 population, live weather and air quality, local food and getaways.</p></div>
